\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The exterior algebra is the regular module}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative ring and let #{d\ge 1}. Write
#{\Lambda} for the exterior algebra of the free module on basis
vectors #{e_1,\ldots,e_d}, and write
#{q\bigl(\sum_i x_i e_i\bigr)=\sum_i x_i^2}. The operators
#{\gamma_i=\epsilon_i+\iota_i} of \ref{fdip-0092} determine a
unital algebra homomorphism from the Clifford algebra
#{\operatorname{Cl}(q)} of \ref{fdip-000P} into the endomorphism
ring of #{\Lambda}. Sending an algebra element to its action on
#{1} is an isomorphism of left modules from
#{\operatorname{Cl}(q)} onto #{\Lambda}. The homomorphism is
injective, and both modules are free of rank #{2^d}. When
#{d=4} that rank is #{16}.}

\proof{
\p{For #{v=\sum_i x_i e_i} set #{C(v)=\sum_i x_i\gamma_i}.
\ref{fdip-0092} gives #{\epsilon_i\iota_i+\iota_i\epsilon_i=I},
hence #{\gamma_i^2=I}, and it gives
#{\gamma_i\gamma_j+\gamma_j\gamma_i=0} for #{i\neq j}. Expanding
#{C(v)^2} leaves #{\sum_i x_i^2 I}, because each cross term
carries an anticommutator. Thus #{C(v)^2=q(v)I}. The universal
property in \ref{fdip-000P} supplies the homomorphism #{\rho}.}

\p{For a subset #{I}, let #{\gamma_I} be the composition of
#{\gamma_i} over #{i\in I}, with the largest index acting first,
and let #{\gamma_\varnothing} be the identity. Applied to #{1},
the contractions meet factors that are not yet present, so each
vanishes, and each exterior multiplication inserts its factor
with sign #{\pm 1}. Hence #{\gamma_I(1)=\pm e_I}. These images
are linearly independent. A linear relation among the
corresponding ordered monomials in #{\operatorname{Cl}(q)} would
give the same relation among the operators #{\gamma_I}, and
evaluating on #{1} would force every coefficient to vanish.}

\p{Products of the generators span #{\operatorname{Cl}(q)}, by
\ref{fdip-000P}. Each generator is a linear combination of the
#{\iota_q(e_i)}, so it is enough to reduce words in that basis.
In such a word, a repeated adjacent generator equals #{1} by the
square relation, and an adjacent inversion may be swapped by the
anticommutator at the cost of a sign. Each swap lowers the number
of inversions and each square shortens the word, so the reduction
ends at a sign times an ordered monomial.
The ordered monomials therefore span. There are #{2^d} of them,
and the previous paragraph shows that they are independent, so
they are a basis.}

\p{The map #{\Phi(a)=\rho(a)(1)} sends that basis to #{\pm e_I}.
The change-of-basis matrix is diagonal with units #{\pm 1} on
the diagonal, so #{\Phi} is a module isomorphism. It preserves
the left action because #{\rho} is an algebra homomorphism.
Injectivity of #{\rho} follows: #{\rho(a)=0} implies
#{\Phi(a)=0}, hence #{a=0}. Both modules are free of rank
#{2^d}. The case #{d=4} is the rank #{16}.}
}
